## Deploy to Google Cloud Run

The service scales to zero, so it costs nothing while idle. Every request needs `Authorization: Bearer <token>`;
the server refuses to start on a public interface without `MCP_AUTH_TOKEN`.

1. Create the secrets once (and grant the service account `roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor`):

   ```bash
   openssl rand -hex 32 | gcloud secrets create <%= it.service %>-auth-token --data-file=-
<% for (const s of it.secrets) { %>   printf %s "$<%= s.env %>" | gcloud secrets create <%= s.secret %> --data-file=-
<% } %>   ```

2. Deploy – either directly from source:

   ```bash
   gcloud run deploy <%= it.service %> --source . --region europe-west1 --allow-unauthenticated \
     --min-instances 0 --max-instances 3 \
     --set-secrets MCP_AUTH_TOKEN=<%= it.service %>-auth-token:latest<% for (const s of it.secrets) { %>,<%= s.env %>=<%= s.secret %>:latest<% } %>
   ```

   or with Skaffold / Cloud Code, using `skaffold.yaml` and `deploy/cloudrun/service.yaml`:

   ```bash
   skaffold run --default-repo=europe-west1-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT/mcp \
     --cloud-run-project=PROJECT --cloud-run-location=europe-west1
   ```

3. Connect your agent to the URL Cloud Run prints:

   ```bash
   claude mcp add --transport http <%= it.name %> https://<%= it.service %>-….run.app/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"
   ```

`--allow-unauthenticated` makes the URL reachable for MCP clients; access is still limited by the bearer token.
With `skaffold run`, grant that once: `gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding <%= it.service %> --member=allUsers --role=roles/run.invoker`.

Prefer Cloud Run IAM over a shared token? IAM uses the same `Authorization` header, so drop the token instead:
deploy without `--allow-unauthenticated`, remove `MCP_AUTH_TOKEN` from the secrets (and from `deploy/cloudrun/service.yaml`)
and set `MCP_ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED=1`. Clients then need an identity token (`gcloud auth print-identity-token`).
